taskDescription = "Sort the array in ascending order using quick sort. Select a pivot element, partition the array so elements smaller than the pivot come before it and larger elements come after it, then recursively sort the partitions."
taskName = "Quick Sort"
taskGuide = "Big O Notation:
- Time Complexity: O(n log n) average case, O(n²) worst case
- Space Complexity: O(log n) for recursion stack

Use Cases:
- General-purpose sorting for large datasets
- When average-case performance is more important than worst-case
- In-place sorting with minimal extra memory
- Cache-friendly algorithm - good locality of reference
- Often faster in practice than other O(n log n) algorithms

Suitable Data Structures:
- Arrays with random access
- Works best when random access is efficient
- Can be adapted for linked lists but less efficient

Algorithm Steps:

1. Choose a pivot element:
   - Common strategy: use the last element in the range
   - Use setPivot(index) to highlight the chosen pivot
   - The pivot will end up in its final sorted position

2. Partition the array around the pivot:
   - Goal: all elements ≤ pivot go to the left
   - All elements > pivot go to the right
   - Pivot ends up between these two groups

3. Partitioning process:
   - Track a boundary between small and large elements
   - Scan through the array comparing each element to pivot
   - When you find an element ≤ pivot, swap it to the left side
   - Use swap(i, j) to move elements

4. Place the pivot in its final position:
   - After scanning, swap pivot with the first large element
   - Pivot is now correctly positioned
   - Everything left of pivot is smaller, everything right is larger

5. Recursively sort the partitions:
   - Apply quick sort to elements left of the pivot
   - Apply quick sort to elements right of the pivot
   - Base case: partition with 0 or 1 elements is already sorted

6. Recursive returns build the solution:
   - Each level of recursion sorts a smaller range
   - When all recursive calls complete, entire array is sorted
   - Call commitSort() to finalize

7. Update pivot visualization:
   - After partitioning, call setPivot(newPivotIndex) 
   - Or setPivot(-1) to clear pivot highlighting

Partitioning Strategy:
The partition step is key - it ensures the pivot is in its correct final position and divides the problem into two smaller independent subproblems."
exampleCode = "# Quick sort
def partition(low, high):
    setPivot(high)
    pivot = array[lb]high[rb]
    i = low - 1
    for j in range(low, high):
        if array[lb]j[rb] <= pivot:
            i += 1
            swap(i, j)
    swap(i + 1, high)
    setPivot(i + 1)
    return i + 1

def quickSort(low, high):
    if low < high:
        pi = partition(low, high)
        setPivot(-1)
        quickSort(low, pi - 1)
        quickSort(pi + 1, high)

quickSort(0, len(array) - 1)
commitSort()"
